{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2217 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "6c1ece525ca3203e4230074ddb942e734c8863d2a219aa48dc2362131afd1db3" :edb/db-before-t 2216 :edb/db-after-t 2217 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535529 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:51:18.377Z" 13194139535529 true] [17592186124217 1100 "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" 13194139535529 false] [17592186124217 1100 "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/f80056f73ec3b3e73a1279990bc8131e6a712af3f62865384a6bd6f6f35595d1.png) is a linearly independent set in $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/d56c111afc375f1cecb7088aba7c67009b5087430e20b84811f9ad45b947df70.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/f0bf564ae35e7aa8bb743034bb915a46ea0ec753ccfd06fb45204201d9b1fa3d.png) is a basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" 13194139535529 true] [17592186124217 1145 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/8c5e7ba97572e7e0a0c548f8877a609e85096c646854bc4c3d2a693943b20496.png)\nis true if and only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/4688890d66d47e92b0e38e603e1b885125dc143a4582b11db92acbf5cdc74ab1.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/697c21a322d1136432b9ac23f959e766f972b829d790d88dc6be4163dce83669.png)\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry $a_{11} = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/0ba338d733e2510ece4a75c2f16acfd1e79f7edbce46232dba8480ab7f37a666.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535529 true] [17592186124217 1145 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true if and only if $x_1=x_2=0$. So, the set is linearly independent.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry $a_{11} = 0$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535529 false] [17592186124218 1100 "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n\n- $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}$ spans $V$\n\n- $\\left\\{  -\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent" 13194139535529 true] [17592186124218 1100 "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2},-\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n2. $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ spans $V$\n3. $\\left\\{-\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent" 13194139535529 false] [17592186124218 1145 "Since $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V$, and\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n$x_{1}b_{2} + x_{2}(-b_{1}) + x_{3}(b_{3} + b_{1})|= 0$\nis equivalent to\n$x_{1}b_{2} + (x_{3} - x_{2})b_{1} + x_{3}b_{3}|= 0$,\nwhich, since $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n${x_{1} = 0; x_{3} - x_{2} = 0; x_{3} = 0$\nAlso, $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2},-\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2},-\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$:\n$x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+x_3\\mathbf{b}_3 = x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+(x_3-x_1)(-\\mathbf{b}_1)+x_3 (\\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1})$\n- Statement II is false. In order for $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ to span $V$, every element of $\\mathcal{B}$ must be able to be expressed as a linear combination of elements of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$. But the vector $\\mathbf{b}_1 \\in \\mathcal{B}$ can't be written as a linear combination of the elements of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$.\n- Statement III is false. If $\\left\\{-\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ not both zero such that\n$x_1(-\\mathbf{b_2}) + x_2(3\\mathbf{b_1}) = \\mathbf{0}$.\nThen, we would have\n$(3x_2) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_1} + (-x_1) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_2} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_3= \\mathbf{0}$.\nBut this would mean that $\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent, which contradicts the fact that $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis of $V$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535529 false] [17592186124218 1145 "Since $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+{x}_{2}(-{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}({b}_{3}+{b}_{1}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/44e05962671a66862ed2fb7a4bc3fc2f859d3a66c56274b869724ee47f6c6414.png)\nAlso, $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+x_3\\mathbf{b}_3\n=\nx_2\\mathbf{b}_2+(x_3-x_1)(-\\mathbf{b}_1)+x_3 (\\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1})\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In order for $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}$ to span $V,$ every element of $\\mathcal{B}$ must be able to be expressed as a linear combination of elements of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}.$ But the vector $\\mathbf{b}_1 \\in \\mathcal{B}$ can't be written as a linear combination of the elements of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If $\\left\\{  -\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ not both zero such that\n\n$$\nx_1(-\\mathbf{b_2}) + x_2(3\\mathbf{b_1}) = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThen, we would have\n\n$$\n(3x_2) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_1} + (-x_1) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_2} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_3= \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nBut this would mean that $\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent, which contradicts the fact that $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis of $V.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535529 true] [17592186124321 1100 "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degrees at most $2$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\big\\{0,t^2 \\big\\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$\n2. $-5t^2$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\big\\{0, t^2 \\big\\}$\n3. $\\big\\{0, t^2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$" 13194139535529 false] [17592186124321 1100 "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\big\\{ 0,t^2 \\big\\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$\n\n- $-5t^2$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\big\\{ 0, t^2 \\big\\}$\n\n- $\\big\\{ 0, t^2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$" 13194139535529 true] [17592186124321 1145 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n$1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0$,\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$-5t^2 = 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + (-5) \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2}$.\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535529 false] [17592186124321 1145 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-5t^2 = 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + (-5) \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535529 true] [17592186124407 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353762 13194139535529 true] [17592186124407 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353818 13194139535529 true] [17592186124407 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353825 13194139535529 true] [17592186124407 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353832 13194139535529 true] [17592186124407 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353839 13194139535529 true] [17592186124408 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353769 13194139535529 true] [17592186124408 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353776 13194139535529 true] [17592186124408 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353783 13194139535529 true] [17592186124408 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353846 13194139535529 true] [17592186124513 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353755 13194139535529 true] [17592186124513 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353790 13194139535529 true] [17592186124513 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353797 13194139535529 true] [17592186124513 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353804 13194139535529 true] [17592186124513 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353811 13194139535529 true] [17592186124772 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535529 true] [17592186124772 1100 "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ and $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}, c_{3}}$ be bases of $R^{2}$ and $R^{3}$, respectively.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. ${b_{1}, b_{2}, x}$ is a basis of $R^{2}$\n2. ${b_{1}, b_{2}}$ is a basis of $R^{3}$\n3. ${c_{3}, c_{2}, c_{1}}$ is a basis of $R^{3}$" 13194139535529 false] [17592186124772 1100 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${{c}_{3},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139535529 true] [17592186124772 1145 "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}.$ This means that ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement III is true. The set ${{c}_{3},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because, by reordering the vectors, we obtain the basis $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535529 true] [17592186353755 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353756 13194139535529 true] [17592186353755 1089 "q-107131" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353755 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535529 true] [17592186353755 1100 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0,1,t}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- $2-t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0,1,t}$\n- ${0,1,t}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353755 1113 #uuid "dd37bb79-8cc2-5705-a5b2-95b0e7ce6d40" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353755 1145 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+0\\cdot t=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n2-t=0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot t.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353756 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353757 13194139535529 true] [17592186353756 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353758 13194139535529 true] [17592186353756 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353759 13194139535529 true] [17592186353756 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353760 13194139535529 true] [17592186353756 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353761 13194139535529 true] [17592186353756 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535529 true] [17592186353756 1041 "selection" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353756 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353761 13194139535529 true] [17592186353756 1110 #uuid "5f140db6-2cc5-58e6-b788-955d18c5715c" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353757 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353757 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535529 true] [17592186353757 1111 #uuid "251235c3-34d8-5100-b030-79c5196351d7" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353758 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353758 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535529 true] [17592186353758 1111 #uuid "338d34cc-f8b7-5657-9d8d-86cc1e555042" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353759 1034 "I only" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353759 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535529 true] [17592186353759 1111 #uuid "02c26099-4a67-5637-8781-7b594e3c9105" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353760 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353760 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535529 true] [17592186353760 1111 #uuid "6b9d3611-2bbc-5219-ac7f-1600ed3d2ac9" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353761 1034 "II only" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353761 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535529 true] [17592186353761 1111 #uuid "6312ae60-e15a-5d5e-b7d6-a4a950ddf526" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353762 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353763 13194139535529 true] [17592186353762 1089 "q-107534" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353762 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535529 true] [17592186353762 1100 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353762 1113 #uuid "e3c5ae8f-24cb-510c-b1a9-706471f11fe3" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353762 1145 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & -a \\\\ 0 & 2b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353764 13194139535529 true] [17592186353763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353765 13194139535529 true] [17592186353763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353766 13194139535529 true] [17592186353763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353767 13194139535529 true] [17592186353763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353768 13194139535529 true] [17592186353763 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535529 true] [17592186353763 1041 "selection" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353763 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353767 13194139535529 true] [17592186353763 1110 #uuid "9c1e847a-36eb-5ad0-b288-e21feeace5fb" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353764 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353764 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535529 true] [17592186353764 1111 #uuid "afc5559e-bf27-5fff-8f9a-79874c8a3881" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353765 1034 "I only" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353765 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535529 true] [17592186353765 1111 #uuid "1663058e-e100-5336-be15-9ff9e6866079" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353766 1034 "III only" 13194139535529 true] [17592186353766 1103 #edb/ref 17592